VERDICT: needs-changes
{"verdict":"needs-changes","summary":"The fix narrows the clear span from 0..visible_rows to top..visible_rows to eliminate a blank band, but rows 0..top retain stale screen content from before the viewport moved down — content that duplicates the first top rows of the redrawn span, trading a blank band for an equally-visible duplication band.","concede":["I previously approved without tracing what content actually remains in rows 0..top after the shrink; re-reading set_height's shrink path confirms no terminal scroll occurs, so those rows hold leftover rendering, not native-scrollback content the diff claims."],"maintain":["The scrollback_committed clamp itself (start = max(naive_start, scrollback_committed)) is correct and necessary — the duplication is purely a clear-span problem, not a draw-span problem."],"findings":[{"file":"crates/mu-solo/src/viewport.rs","line":648,"severity":"medium","issue":"Clearing only top..visible_rows leaves rows 0..top with stale content from before the viewport moved down. That stale content is history[scrollback_committed..scrollback_committed+top], which is the same content redrawn at rows top..2*top — producing a visible duplication band of height top (= new_y - old_y), the same height as the blank band the fix replaces. Concrete trace: history_len=100, old_y=30 (scrollback_committed=70), new_y=40 → top=10; rows 0..9 still show history[70..80] from the pre-shrink rendering, while rows 10..19 are redrawn with history[70..80] — an exact duplicate. The unit tests only verify clear_span==draw_span and never check what rows 0..top contain, giving false confidence."}]}
